{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["But <b>if she is ra'ah</b> — unattractive, as in 'why are your faces ra'im today' — in the eyes of her master, to designate her to remain in his house until her appointed time, when her years of service are completed." "<b>Designate her (ye'edeha).</b> From the root מועד, as in 'to the appointed place (mo'ed) that they shall designate (ye'edu).' Written <b>lo ye'edeha</b> (with vav), read as לו (with alef, third-person feminine: 'he shall designate her')." "<b>And redeem her.</b> And to redeem her — when the master does not wish to designate her and also does not wish her to redeem herself, but wants her to remain with him in degradation like a regular maidservant, and she does not want this." "<b>And redeem her.</b> Rashi's comment: that he too assists in her redemption, etc. And if you ask: what assistance, and what deduction does she have from her master? One may answer: it is established that she is reckoned to perform all labor and all kinds of servitude in the last three years more than she was at the beginning of the first three years, for she was young when first sold for the first years. Thus it is established: <b>and redeem her</b> — when she leaves his domain, he does not reckon with her according to what she was worth in the last three years, but rather deducts from her redemption for the years she already served him, and she returns to him for the remaining years, per each year, one-sixth of the silver." "<b>To a foreign people.</b> To another man who is not her master." "<b>He shall not have dominion to sell her.</b> Even to designate her for himself as a wife. 'To sell her' — to hand her over. As in, 'for by the hand of a woman HaShem will sell Sisera.'" "<b>If he has betrayed her.</b> That he does not want to marry her. The term 'betrayal' applies in the matter of a man's separation from a woman, as in, 'whom you have betrayed, your companion and the wife of your covenant.' And it says, 'as a woman betrays her companion.' And one may say, <b>to a foreign people</b> — to his Canaanite servant, in order to beget children from her to serve him, in the manner that one hands over a Canaanite maidservant to his Hebrew servant, for in this matter there is great blemish and disgrace. <b>To a foreign people</b> — a Canaanite is called 'foreign,' as it is written, 'to a foreign city who are not of the children of Israel are they.' And it says, 'and the foreigner who is not of your people comes.' And one may say, <b>and she shall be redeemed</b> — her father shall redeem her from under the hand of that master, since he does not want to designate her." "<b>To a foreign people.</b> To the nations of the world — the father shall not have dominion to sell her, so that one might not say: since he was given permission to sell her, he may sell her even to a Canaanite, in the manner that a man sells himself, as it is written, 'or to the root of the family of a stranger' — therefore it says <b>he shall not have dominion to sell her</b> — this is a warning that she not be sold to a Canaanite." "<b>If he has betrayed her.</b> That he should not have sold his daughter, yet sold her."], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {3 "The comment names Rashi explicitly, which is unusual for Chizkuni but present in this passage; preserved as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:59:26.493697006Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}